course: dictionary
lesson: 5069
title: Instantly and at once
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Instantly means at once, with no time at all in between. When you meet it, ask: how quickly is this happening? «An open oven door is hot enough to burn skin instantly.» The clue is burn: the skin is hurt straight away, not after a while.
explain: Some words show how fast something is. «Light reaches you almost instantly» means light is very quick, but almost tells you it is not quite zero time. Look for words like almost or nearly near instantly to see how fast it is.
explain: Speed can be checked with numbers. «Sound travels only about 1 kilometre in 3 seconds.» Light arrives almost instantly, so when you see a flash, the bang comes late. That gap tells you how far away the storm is.
fact: Instantly means at once, in an instant, with no time at all in between.
fact: «Almost instantly» means very, very fast, but not quite at once.
fact: «Instantly recognisable» means you know something straight away from one look or one sound.
fact: Light reaches you almost instantly; sound takes much longer, about 1 kilometre every 3 seconds.
fact: A good check is to swap in at once: if the sentence still makes sense, instantly has the right meaning.
exercise: «The lights came on instantly when she closed the circuit.» What does instantly mean? | Only sometimes | Slowly, after a long wait | *At once, with no time in between | Loudly and with a bang
exercise: «Nothing heavy can stop instantly: a car needs time and distance.» What does this sentence tell you? | *Heavy things can stop at once, with no time needed | Heavy things need time and distance to stop | Trains are always silent | Heavy things never move
exercise: Which word could swap with instantly in «He recognised her instantly»? | *At once | Sometimes | Never | Slowly
test: «Phone filters find your face in real time, which means instantly while you film.» What does instantly mean here? | Upside down | *As you film, at once | Long after you finish filming | Only at night
test: «Light reaches you almost instantly, but sound travels only about 1 kilometre in 3 seconds.» Which arrives first? | Sound | Neither arrives | They always arrive at the same time | *Light
exam: What does instantly mean, and how can you check it? | *At once; swap in at once and see if the sentence still fits | Far away; swap in far and see if it fits | Quietly; swap in softly and see if it fits | Late; swap in later and see if it fits
